PATCH: zephyr_sockets_recvq_close_race_leak — drop pkt that races socket close
==============================================================================

Written against: Zephyr 4.4.x  (verified present in v4.4.1 and main, 2026-06)
Target platform: ESP32-S3 on Zephyr RTOS, TCP/TLS sockets
Application:     /workspace/projects/hello_eink  (LLSS e-ink client)

SUMMARY
-------
net_context_packet_received() validates context->recv_cb under context->lock
then invokes the receive callback AFTER unlocking. zsock_close() concurrently
clears recv_cb (net_context_recv(NULL)) and then drains ctx->recv_q via
zsock_flush_queue(). A segment that passed the recv_cb!=NULL check and unlocked
gets enqueued into ctx->recv_q by zsock_received_cb() AFTER the flush already
ran. Nothing re-drains recv_q (tcp_conn_release() drains only conn->recv_data),
so the net_pkt is stranded for the life of the system. Under abrupt closes
(e.g. closing a TLS socket on an HTTP error) this slowly exhausts the RX pool.

ROOT CAUSE / FIX
----------------
Guard the enqueue: if recv_cb is NULL when the callback runs, the socket is
closing and the data would never be read, so unref the pkt instead of queuing
it. Safe because zsock_close() clears recv_cb BEFORE flushing recv_q: recv_cb
non-NULL => flush hasn't run => enqueue will be drained; recv_cb NULL => flush
ran/won't re-run => drop. Confirmed on hardware (40 races = 40 leaks = full RX
pool pre-fix; 0 leaks post-fix). Full writeup: NETBUF_LEAK_ADVENTURE.md.

--- 8< --- diff --- 8< ---
--- a/subsys/net/lib/sockets/sockets_inet.c	2026-06-20 13:19:28.007176259 +0000
+++ b/subsys/net/lib/sockets/sockets_inet.c	2026-06-20 13:19:28.007406730 +0000
@@ -280,6 +280,20 @@
 
 	net_pkt_set_rx_stats_tick(pkt, k_cycle_get_32());
 
+	/* Close-race guard: net_context_packet_received() validates recv_cb
+	 * under context->lock then invokes this callback AFTER unlocking.
+	 * zsock_close() NULLs recv_cb (net_context_recv(NULL)) BEFORE
+	 * zsock_flush_queue() drains recv_q. So recv_cb==NULL here means a
+	 * close has raced in and the flush has already run (or will not
+	 * re-run): enqueueing now strands this pkt in recv_q forever
+	 * (nothing re-drains recv_q; tcp_conn_release() drains only
+	 * conn->recv_data). Drop it -- the socket is closed and the app will
+	 * never read it, same outcome as the flush. */
+	if (ctx->recv_cb == NULL) {
+		net_pkt_unref(pkt);
+		goto unlock;
+	}
+
 	k_fifo_put(&ctx->recv_q, pkt);
 
 unlock:
